@startuml error_flow_redis_failure

title COOKie MVP - Redis Cache Failure Handling Flow\nGraceful Degradation & Circuit Breaker Pattern

actor "Client" as Client
participant "Controller" as Controller
participant "Cache Service\n(ICacheService)" as CacheService
database "Redis Cache" as Redis
database "PostgreSQL" as DB
participant "Circuit Breaker\n(Polly)" as CircuitBreaker
participant "Serilog\nLogger" as Logger
participant "Health Check\nService" as HealthCheck

== Scenario 1: Redis Unavailable - Graceful Degradation ==

Client -> Controller: GET /api/v1/recipes/borsch-classic
activate Controller

Controller -> CacheService: get("recipe:borsch-classic")
activate CacheService

CacheService -> CircuitBreaker: Check circuit state
activate CircuitBreaker

alt Circuit Breaker: CLOSED (normal operation)
  CircuitBreaker --> CacheService: Circuit CLOSED, proceed
  deactivate CircuitBreaker

  CacheService -> Redis: GET recipe:borsch-classic
  activate Redis

  Redis --> CacheService: Error: Connection refused\n(Redis server down)
  deactivate Redis

  CacheService -> Logger: Log.Warning(\n  "Redis connection failed",\n  operation: "GET",\n  key: "recipe:borsch-classic",\n  error: "Connection refused"\n)

  CacheService -> CircuitBreaker: Record failure\n(failure_count: 1 of 5)

  CacheService --> Controller: CacheMissResult(\n  reason: "connection_error"\n)
  deactivate CacheService

  Controller -> Controller: Fallback: query database directly

  Controller -> DB: SELECT * FROM recipes\nWHERE slug = 'borsch-classic'\nAND status = 'published'
  activate DB
  DB --> Controller: Recipe data
  deactivate DB

  Controller -> Controller: Skip cache write\n(Redis is down)

  Controller --> Client: 200 OK\nX-Cache: BYPASS\nX-Cache-Reason: connection-error\n{\n  "id": "uuid-1",\n  "slug": "borsch-classic",\n  "title": "Классический борщ",\n  ...\n}
  deactivate Controller

else Circuit Breaker: OPEN (Redis known to be down)
  CircuitBreaker --> CacheService: Circuit OPEN\n(skip Redis entirely)
  deactivate CircuitBreaker

  CacheService -> Logger: Log.Debug(\n  "Circuit breaker OPEN, skipping Redis"\n)

  CacheService --> Controller: CacheMissResult(\n  reason: "circuit_open"\n)
  deactivate CacheService

  Controller -> DB: Query database directly\n(bypass cache entirely)
  activate DB
  DB --> Controller: Recipe data
  deactivate DB

  Controller --> Client: 200 OK\nX-Cache: BYPASS\nX-Cache-Reason: circuit-open\n{\n  "id": "uuid-1",\n  ...\n}
  deactivate Controller
end

== Scenario 2: Redis Slow Response (Timeout) ==

Client -> Controller: GET /api/v1/recipes?q=pasta&page=1
activate Controller

Controller -> CacheService: get("recipes:search:hash-abc")
activate CacheService

CacheService -> Redis: GET recipes:search:hash-abc\n(timeout: 100ms)
activate Redis

Redis -> Redis: Processing...\n(slow due to high load)

note over Redis
  Response takes > 100ms
  (configured timeout threshold)
end note

Redis --> CacheService: Timeout after 100ms\n(response too slow)
deactivate Redis

CacheService -> Logger: Log.Warning(\n  "Redis timeout exceeded",\n  operation: "GET",\n  timeout_ms: 100,\n  key: "recipes:search:hash-abc"\n)

CacheService -> CircuitBreaker: Record failure\n(slow response = failure)

CacheService --> Controller: CacheMissResult(\n  reason: "timeout"\n)
deactivate CacheService

Controller -> DB: Execute search query directly\n(full-text search)
activate DB
DB --> Controller: Search results
deactivate DB

Controller -> CacheService: set("recipes:search:hash-abc", results, TTL=300)
activate CacheService

CacheService -> Redis: SETEX recipes:search:hash-abc ...\n(attempt write, timeout: 50ms)
activate Redis

alt Write Succeeds
  Redis --> CacheService: OK
  deactivate Redis
  CacheService --> Controller: Cached successfully
  deactivate CacheService
else Write Fails (still slow)
  Redis --> CacheService: Timeout
  deactivate Redis
  CacheService -> Logger: Log.Warning("Cache write timeout, skipping")
  CacheService --> Controller: Write skipped (non-blocking)
  deactivate CacheService
end

Controller --> Client: 200 OK\nX-Cache: MISS\n{\n  "recipes": [...],\n  "pagination": {...}\n}
deactivate Controller

== Scenario 3: Cache Corruption / Invalid Data ==

Client -> Controller: GET /api/v1/recipes/popular
activate Controller

Controller -> CacheService: get("recipes:popular")
activate CacheService

CacheService -> Redis: GET recipes:popular
activate Redis

Redis --> CacheService: Corrupted JSON:\n"{\\"recipes\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"...\n(truncated/invalid)"
deactivate Redis

CacheService -> CacheService: Attempt JSON deserialization\n→ JsonException thrown

CacheService -> Logger: Log.Warning(\n  "Cache data corrupted",\n  key: "recipes:popular",\n  error: "Invalid JSON"\n)

CacheService -> Redis: DEL recipes:popular\n(invalidate corrupted entry)
activate Redis
Redis --> CacheService: OK (key deleted)
deactivate Redis

CacheService --> Controller: CacheMissResult(\n  reason: "data_corrupted"\n)
deactivate CacheService

Controller -> DB: SELECT * FROM recipes\nORDER BY popularity_score DESC\nLIMIT 20
activate DB
DB --> Controller: Popular recipes
deactivate DB

Controller -> CacheService: set("recipes:popular", results, TTL=900)
activate CacheService
CacheService -> Redis: SETEX recipes:popular ... 900
activate Redis
Redis --> CacheService: OK
deactivate Redis
CacheService --> Controller: Cached (rebuilt from DB)
deactivate CacheService

Controller --> Client: 200 OK\nX-Cache: REBUILT\n{\n  "recipes": [...]\n}
deactivate Controller

== Scenario 4: Redis Recovery & Cache Warming ==

HealthCheck -> Redis: PING\n(every 10 seconds)
activate Redis

alt Redis Recovers After Outage
  Redis --> HealthCheck: PONG (healthy)
  deactivate Redis

  HealthCheck -> CircuitBreaker: Reset circuit\n(transition: OPEN → HALF-OPEN)

  HealthCheck -> Logger: Log.Information(\n  "Redis recovered, circuit half-open",\n  downtime_seconds: 120\n)

  note over HealthCheck, Redis
    **HALF-OPEN State:**
    Allow a limited number of requests through
    to test if Redis is truly recovered.
    - Allow 3 test requests
    - If all succeed → CLOSED (normal)
    - If any fail → back to OPEN
  end note

  HealthCheck -> CacheService: Trigger cache warming\n(background task)
  activate CacheService

  CacheService -> DB: SELECT * FROM recipes\nORDER BY popularity_score DESC\nLIMIT 20
  activate DB
  DB --> CacheService: Top 20 popular recipes
  deactivate DB

  CacheService -> Redis: SETEX recipes:popular ...\n(warm critical cache keys)
  activate Redis
  Redis --> CacheService: OK
  deactivate Redis

  CacheService -> DB: SELECT DISTINCT slug FROM cuisines
  activate DB
  DB --> CacheService: Cuisine list
  deactivate DB

  CacheService -> Redis: SETEX recipes:cuisines ...
  activate Redis
  Redis --> CacheService: OK
  deactivate Redis

  CacheService -> Logger: Log.Information(\n  "Cache warming complete",\n  keys_warmed: 2\n)
  deactivate CacheService

  HealthCheck -> CircuitBreaker: All test requests OK\n→ Transition: HALF-OPEN → CLOSED
  HealthCheck -> Logger: Log.Information(\n  "Redis fully recovered, circuit CLOSED"\n)
end

== Circuit Breaker Configuration ==

note over CacheService, CircuitBreaker
  **Circuit Breaker Settings (Polly):**

  **CLOSED → OPEN Transition:**
  - Failure threshold: 5 failures in 30 seconds
  - Failure types: ConnectionException, TimeoutException
  - Excludes: cache miss (null result is normal)

  **OPEN State Duration:**
  - Break duration: 30 seconds
  - During OPEN: all cache reads return miss immediately
  - No requests sent to Redis (zero latency impact)

  **OPEN → HALF-OPEN Transition:**
  - After 30 seconds of OPEN state
  - Allow 3 test requests through
  - Monitor success rate

  **HALF-OPEN → CLOSED:**
  - All 3 test requests succeed → CLOSED
  - Any failure → back to OPEN for 30 more seconds

  **Metrics Tracked:**
  - circuit_state: closed | open | half_open
  - failure_count: rolling 30-second window
  - bypass_count: requests that skipped Redis
  - recovery_time: seconds until circuit closes
end note

== Cache Service Interface ==

note over Controller, CacheService
  **ICacheService Interface:**

  ```
  interface ICacheService {
    Task<T?> GetAsync<T>(string key);
    Task SetAsync<T>(string key, T value, TimeSpan ttl);
    Task RemoveAsync(string key);
    Task RemoveByPatternAsync(string pattern);
    CacheHealthStatus GetHealthStatus();
  }
  ```

  **Cache Operation Rules:**
  1. Cache READ failure → NEVER fail the request
  2. Cache WRITE failure → log warning, continue
  3. Cache operations are fire-and-forget for writes
  4. Always set TTL (no infinite cache keys)
  5. Use consistent key naming: entity:identifier

  **Cache Key Naming Convention:**
  - recipe:{slug} → single recipe
  - recipes:search:{hash} → search results
  - recipes:popular → popular recipes list
  - recipes:cuisines → cuisine list
  - user:{id}:favorites → user's favorites
  - user:{id}:profile → user profile
  - auth:blacklist:{jti} → revoked token
end note

== Performance Impact ==

note over Client, DB
  **With Redis (normal operation):**
  - Cache hit: ~5ms response time
  - Cache miss: ~60ms (DB query + cache write)
  - Cache hit ratio: ~80%
  - Average response: ~16ms

  **Without Redis (degraded):**
  - All requests hit DB: ~60ms
  - No caching overhead: save ~2ms per request
  - DB load increases ~5x
  - Average response: ~60ms

  **Mitigation for DB Load Spike:**
  - Connection pool handles burst (max 100)
  - Query timeout prevents slow queries
  - Popular recipes query is indexed
  - Consider read replicas for scaling (Phase 2)

  **Response Headers for Monitoring:**
  - X-Cache: HIT | MISS | BYPASS | REBUILT
  - X-Cache-Reason: (only on BYPASS)
    - connection-error
    - timeout
    - circuit-open
    - data_corrupted
  - X-Cache-TTL: remaining seconds (on HIT)
end note

